1. INTRODUCTION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1.1 The cosmological constant problem
-------------------------------------

The Standard Model does not predict the cosmological constant. A naive
estimate of the vacuum energy density from quantum field theory gives

    rho_vac ~ M_P^4 ~ 10^76 GeV^4,

roughly 10^120 times larger than the observed dark energy density

    rho_Lambda ~ 2.5e-47 GeV^4.

This is the cosmological constant problem.

HCSM resolves the problem in two stages. First (HCSM-53), the framework
derives the VALUE of the residual vacuum energy from mirror cancellation on
the 8x8 torus: the Laplacian spectrum is exactly mirror-symmetric under
lambda <-> 8 - lambda, so 50 of the 64 modes pair into cancelling partners,
and only the 14 self-paired modes at lambda = 4 survive. The residual is
rho_DE = (d+1)/2 * m_nu^4, matching observation.

Second (this sub-paper), the framework derives the PHYSICAL MECHANISM by
which the residual is promoted to the observed cosmological constant.

1.2 The promotion problem
-------------------------

Even given the correct numerical value rho_DE, the framework must explain
HOW the residual vacuum energy on the 14-mode multiplet becomes the
observed cosmological constant in 4D spacetime. Two mechanisms are
conceivable:

    (1) A PROJECTION: the EFT layer of the five-layer loop acts as a soft
        projector onto the residual subspace, and the residual energy is
        projected onto the timelike direction of ker D.

    (2) A COUPLING: the residual energy is coupled to gravity through a
        suppression factor 1/M_P^2, giving rho_obs ~ rho_res / M_P^2.

The first predicts rho_obs ≈ rho_res; the second predicts a huge
suppression. Observation shows rho_obs / rho_res ≈ 1.0077 (using the
refined formula), which is consistent with a projection with weight
1 + c*delta = 1.007808. The mechanism is projection.

