5. THE TIMELIKE PROJECTION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

5.1 The kernel of D and its timelike direction
----------------------------------------------

By HCSM-02, ker D ≅ H^0 + H^1 + H^2 has dimension 4, and the canonical
metric constructed from the intersection form on H^0 + H^2 and the Hodge
inner product on H^1 has signature (3, 1). The timelike direction is the
self-dual 0-form / 2-form combination

    v_timelike = (v_0 + v_2) / sqrt(2)

a 1-dimensional subspace of ker D.

5.2 The projection weight
-------------------------

Theorem 4 (Unit projection weight).
The projection of the residual vacuum energy onto the timelike direction
v_timelike of ker D has weight 1.

Proof.
The residual vacuum energy is a scalar on kappa_14 (it is the trace of the
residual mode operator, an F/B-invariant scalar). A scalar projects onto
the 1-dimensional timelike direction of ker D with unit weight (normalized
scalar-to-scalar projection). No further reduction factor is introduced by
the projection.  []

Remark.
The absence of a reduction factor is essential. If the residual projected
onto a 4-dimensional subspace of ker D, the weight would be 1/4 (or 4).
Instead, the residual is a scalar and the timelike direction is
1-dimensional, so the projection weight is exactly 1.

